      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical States (The Phases of Water)\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in three distinct states depending on the temperature:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard state of water at room temperature, found in rivers, oceans, and rain.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Ice is uniquely **less dense than liquid water**, which is why ice floats. \n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the atmosphere.\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances. Because of its chemical structure, it easily bonds with salts, sugars, minerals, and gases. This is how our blood transports nutrients through our bodies, and how oceans stay salty.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a massive amount of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This property allows Earth’s oceans to regulate the planet's climate and prevents coastal areas from experiencing extreme temperature swings.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (**cohesion**—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (**adhesion**—which allows water to travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Abundance:** Water covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** About 96.5% of Earth's water is salt water in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is fresh water, and most of that is locked away"
